/target
.env

# The corpus, and everything shaped like a piece of it.
#
# `docs/RISKS.md` R3 is about an irreversibility that git makes literal: a
# recording committed once is in the history and in every fork, and deleting the
# file afterwards does not delete it. Retroactive consent from somebody who
# played six months ago is, in practice, not obtainable, so the only workable
# rule is that this never happens once.
#
# These patterns are the accident-proofing, not the policy: the corpus lives in
# a directory outside the repository entirely (`docs/CONSENT.md`). The `ci`
# check that fails on a *tracked* file of these shapes is what catches a
# `git add -f`, which is the only way past a `.gitignore`.
/corpus/
*.replay
*.consent
*.identity
*.withdrawn

# M6 adds two shapes and neither is less personal than a replay. A session
# record describes what somebody's match was played on — their mouse, their
# platform, their machine's ability to keep the tick — and a session part is one
# seat's half of it as the client wrote it out. Neither carries a pseudonym, and
# that is exactly why they are here: `Corpus::audit` finds a withdrawn
# participant by searching for their name, so a file that names nobody is a file
# no audit of any pseudonym would ever report as committed.
*.session
*.session-part

# And the telemetry companion, which is the most personal file in the corpus and
# the one that names nobody at all. It is the movement of somebody's hand at
# their mouse's own report rate — `docs/SCHEMA.md` §11 — and, like the two
# shapes above, a search for a pseudonym would never find it committed because
# there is no pseudonym in it. The part is one seat's half of it as the client
# wrote it out, before anything has sealed anything.
*.telemetry
*.telemetry-part

# The replay signing key, which arrives at M5.
#
# A different kind of secret from the corpus and refused for a different reason:
# whoever holds it can seal a manifest this project's own verifier accepts, so a
# committed one is a committed authority to mint evidence — and, like a
# recording, a key committed once is in the history and in every fork and
# deleting the file does not delete it. `replay keygen` writes the pair and says
# so on the way out.
#
# The **public** half is deliberately not here. `docs/RISKS.md` R4 requires every
# key, including every retired one, to stay published: a retired key that stops
# being published orphans every replay it ever sealed, which is a way of
# destroying evidence by housekeeping. So `*.public-key` is a file this
# repository is meant to be able to carry.
*.signing-key
